      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The casting of Chambers was a deliberate provocation that paralleled the film's themes of sexual transmission and bodily autonomy. Her performance bridges exploitation cinema and serious horror, much like Barbara Steele in <a href=\"/movie/27632\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"27632\">Black Sunday</a> (1960) or Linnea Quigley in <a href=\"/movie/10925\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"10925\">Return of the Living Dead</a> (1985). The film's Montreal outbreak scenario eerily foreshadows contemporary viral anxiety, while its body horror elements influenced films like <a href=\"/movie/9035\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"9035\">Slither</a> (2006) and Contracted (2013)."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The surgical scenes and medical context place Rabid within a specific subset of medical horror that includes <a href=\"/movie/37169\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"37169\">The Human Centipede</a> (2009) and <a href=\"/movie/1694\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"1694\">Re-Animator</a> (1985). Cronenberg's clinical approach to gore and transformation would later influence directors like Stuart Gordon and Clive Barker, particularly in films like <a href=\"/movie/9003\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"9003\">Hellraiser</a> (1987) and <a href=\"/movie/14510\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"14510\">From Beyond</a> (1986). SUBHEAD: Evolution of Viral Horror"
      },
      {
        "type": "movies",
        "movies": [
          {
            "title": "Videodrome",
            "year": 1983,
            "slug": "James Woods discovers a sinister broadcast signal that causes hallucinations and physical transformation",
